# dotfiles-Gentoo/gentoo/package.accept_keywords
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# INSTALLED, not just documented. bootstrap.sh renders __ARCH__ from
# `portageq envvar ARCH` and writes this to:
#
#     /etc/portage/package.accept_keywords/90-dotfiles-Gentoo
#
# Skip it with `./bootstrap.sh --no-portage-config`, and remove it by deleting
# that one namespaced file — nothing here edits a file Portage or another package
# owns.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT OPTIONAL. On a stable profile, several tools in this stack have
# NO stable-keyworded version in ::gentoo at all, so `emerge` refuses them outright
# and `core doctor` reports a permanent ✗. Verified on 23.0/desktop, re-checked
# 2026-08-26:
#
#     app-shells/zoxide     5 ebuilds, 0 with a stable amd64 keyword
#     sys-fs/duf            2 ebuilds, 0 with a stable amd64 keyword
#     dev-util/difftastic   2 ebuilds, 0 with a stable amd64 keyword
#
# dev-util/shellcheck was the fourth and is gone from this file on purpose — see
# the shellcheck note at the bottom before adding it back.
#
# THERE ARE NOW TWO KINDS OF LINE HERE, and the difference matters when you read a
# line and ask whether it is still needed:
#
#   unreachable — no stable ebuild exists, so the atom cannot install at all. The
#                 four above, plus dev-vcs/jj and every GURU atom.
#   below-floor — the atom installs fine on stable, but the stable version is under
#                 a minimum Core requires. Version-restricted (`>=atom-x.y.z`) so
#                 that older versions keep tracking stable. One today: neovim.
#
# Everything in the GURU overlay is testing-keyworded by convention, which is the
# other ten. Shipping this list commented out — as this file did — meant a fresh
# box finished "complete" while silently skipping a quarter of the stack.
#
# WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO: blanket-unmask. There is no `*/* ~amd64`
# line here and there should never be one. Each atom is listed because this repo
# installs it and it needs the keyword; an atom that emerges fine on stable is
# absent on purpose, so it keeps tracking stable.
#
# To review before it lands:  ./bootstrap.sh --dry-run
# To see why one atom is blocked:  emerge -p <category/name>
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── ::gentoo, but testing-only ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No stable keyword exists for these; without the line they cannot install at all.
app-shells/zoxide ~__ARCH__
sys-fs/duf ~__ARCH__

# ── ::gentoo stable, but BELOW a floor Core requires ──────────────────────────
# NOT a reachability line: app-editors/neovim installs perfectly well on a stable
# profile. It installs the WRONG THING. The stable head is 0.11.7 and Core's
# nvim-treesitter is pinned to `main`, which hard-requires 0.12 — so without this
# line a stable box emerges 0.11.7, reports success, and Core's nvim config does not
# load. "Resolves" and "usable" are different questions; this file only ever
# answered the first one until now.
#
# Version-restricted on purpose. A bare `app-editors/neovim ~__ARCH__` would work,
# but it unmasks 0.11.x testing releases too, and it reads as an unreachable-atom
# line — which check 4 would then report as dead weight ("has gone stable, drop
# it") about the one line here that must NOT be dropped. The `>=` form states the
# floor, and check 8 matches it against `# min:0.12.0` in install/packages.txt.
>=app-editors/neovim-0.12.0 ~__ARCH__

# ── the DEPENDENCY of a below-floor atom, which is a third kind of line ────────
# Unmasking an atom does not unmask what it needs, and nothing in this file said so
# until issue #133. EVERY app-editors/neovim 0.12.x in ::gentoo requires
# >=dev-lua/luv-1.52.1, and the only luv at that version (1.52.1.0) is ~arch on every
# arch in the tree — so the neovim line above, on its own, has NEVER been enough. A
# fresh stable box read it, selected neovim 0.12.3, and failed on luv.
#
# Every per-atom check in scripts/check-packages.sh passed on this the whole time,
# and could not have done otherwise: check 3 asks whether NEOVIM is keyworded and
# check 8 asks whether the keyword line reaches the `# min:` floor. Both are about
# neovim. Neither can see one level down. It took Portage's own resolver
# (the `emerge -p` step in .github/workflows/packages.yml) to find it — which is the
# argument for keeping that step and promoting it to fatal.
#
# Version-restricted for the same reason neovim's line is: the older luv ebuilds are
# stable and should keep tracking stable.
>=dev-lua/luv-1.52.1 ~__ARCH__

# ── ::gentoo dev/repo tooling, testing-only ───────────────────────────────────
# NB there is NO dev-util/shellcheck line here, and adding one would be the bug
# issue #133 was filed about. That atom is Haskell: it needs >=dev-haskell/aeson-1.4.0
# and the rest of the GHC chain, all ~arch, so keywording shellcheck ALONE unmasks
# nothing and the emerge still fails — while the file you were told to edit now
# carries the entry you were told to add. install/packages.txt lists
# dev-util/shellcheck-bin instead, which is STABLE on amd64/arm64 and needs no line.
dev-util/difftastic ~__ARCH__

# ── ::gentoo, opt-in extras block in bootstrap.sh ─────────────────────────────
# NOT from install/packages.txt: bootstrap.sh's opt-in extras block emerges this
# one (skipped by --no-extras), so it is the only atom in this file whose source
# list lives in the script rather than in packages.txt. scripts/check-packages.sh
# check 7 reads it from the extras_install call, the same way checks 5-6 read the
# guru_install list.
#
# ::gentoo gained dev-vcs/jj on 2025-12-04. All four ebuilds (0.40.0 - 0.44.0)
# are KEYWORDS="~amd64" with no stable version, so without this line the emerge
# is refused outright — the same shape as zoxide and duf above.
dev-vcs/jj ~__ARCH__

# ── GURU overlay, opt-in extras block in bootstrap.sh ─────────────────────────
# The only atom here that is BOTH overlay-sourced and opt-in (skipped by
# --no-extras), which is why bootstrap.sh has a third seam for it —
# guru_extras_install — rather than putting it in the unconditional guru_install
# list. scripts/check-packages.sh check 9 reads it from that call.
#
# It was a `cargo install --locked ouch` until issue #133: that build fails on any
# GCC/libstdc++ box (its default unrar feature pulls unrar-ng-sys, whose build.rs
# hard-codes -stdlib=libc++), and GURU's ebuild is both at upstream's latest version
# and already patches that flag out. See install/packages.txt for the full note.
app-arch/ouch ~__ARCH__

# ── GURU overlay ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GURU ships ebuilds testing-keyworded as a matter of policy, so every atom
# bootstrap.sh emerges from it needs a line here. These are the core-doctor tools
# absent from the main tree (see PORTING-MATRIX.md footnote 12).
sys-apps/sd ~__ARCH__
app-misc/glow ~__ARCH__
net-misc/xh ~__ARCH__
app-shells/carapace ~__ARCH__
app-misc/1password-cli ~__ARCH__
app-misc/tealdeer ~__ARCH__
app-misc/yazi ~__ARCH__
dev-vcs/lazygit ~__ARCH__
app-shells/direnv ~__ARCH__
net-analyzer/gping ~__ARCH__

# NB: an ebuild carrying NO keywords at all (some live/9999 ebuilds, and the
# occasional GURU package) is not reached by `~arch` — it needs `**`. The same is
# true of a LEADING `-*`, which is a stronger statement than "untested here": it
# masks every arch not named explicitly, and `~arch` cannot reach through it.
# dev-util/shellcheck-bin (KEYWORDS="-* amd64 ~arm arm64") is the one atom this
# repo installs that has one, so on x86, ppc64 or riscv it is reachable only with
# `**` — nothing here requires that today, because the fleet is amd64/arm64.
#
# If an atom is still masked after this file lands, run `emerge -p <atom>`: it
# prints the exact keyword or licence that is missing — AND, when the blocker is a
# DEPENDENCY rather than the atom itself, it names that dependency instead. Read
# which atom the "have been masked" line actually quotes before editing this file;
# bootstrap.sh's own skip hint now does that for you.
